Convert the validated scenarios into detailed, executable test cases using only the supplied scenarios and source context.
Rules:
1. Do not invent application behavior, UI labels, endpoints, fields, workflows, or IDs.
2. Preserve scenario, project, requirement, and acceptance-criterion IDs exactly.
3. Include clear preconditions and realistic, specific test data.
4. Include consecutively ordered executable steps and a measurable expected result for every step.
5. Ensure each test case validates its scenario objective and linked acceptance criteria.
6. Do not calculate confidence scores or add unsupported fields.
7. Omit test_case_id so the server assigns a UUID. Copy scenario_id and project_id exactly.
8. Number steps consecutively beginning at 1.
9. Return JSON only, with no markdown or explanation, matching {"test_cases": [{"scenario_id": "EXACT_SCENARIO_ID_UUID", "project_id": "EXACT_PROJECT_ID_UUID", "title": "Test case title", "description": "Detailed description", "test_case_type": "functional", "steps": [{"step_number": 1, "action": "Executable action step", "expected_result": "Expected outcome"}]}]}. Every item in test_cases MUST be a full JSON object, NEVER a string or raw text.
10. Use compact visual context only for visible UI interactions and accessibility checks; textual requirements remain authoritative.
11. Do not infer authorization, API, database, performance, or security behavior from screenshots alone.
12. Every test_cases[] item MUST populate these required fields: scenario_id, project_id, title, description, test_case_type, and steps.
13. steps MUST be a non-empty array. Every steps[] item MUST contain step_number, a non-empty action, and a non-empty expected_result. Never omit steps and never return an empty steps array.
14. Return exactly one complete test case for every supplied scenario. Do not return partial test-case objects.
15. All input steps (fill, enter, type, select) MUST contain explicit executable input values enclosed in quotes (e.g. 'Fill "Name" with "John Doe"' or 'Enter "Email" with "user@example.com"'). Never generate vague actions like "Enter a valid name". Use concrete test data for positive input actions and explicit empty strings (e.g. 'Fill "Name" with ""') for negative/empty-field tests.
16. Do not invent post-registration or post-action expected behavior. Expected results must be supported by acceptance criteria or actual page evidence; otherwise mark the expected result or step BLOCKED.
17. Strict Deduplication: Generate exactly one unique test case for each distinct scenario. Never repeat or output duplicate test cases.
18. Real Application Crawl Grounding: Use discovered application knowledge (pages, page titles, accessible names, element roles, placeholders, test IDs, and verified locators) whenever possible. Never generate generic vague steps like "Click the appropriate button" when the real button name is known. Generate grounded executable actions like "Navigate to Appointments and click the 'Auto Renew' button". Expected results remain requirement-driven (User Story + AC determine WHAT to verify; Application Knowledge determines HOW to interact).
19. Correct Navigation Flow & Unsupported Evidence: Steps must follow the actual discovered application navigation flow (e.g. Login -> Navigate to Target Page -> Perform Action -> Verify Result). Do NOT generate steps referencing an element before navigating to its containing page. If an action or element required by the acceptance criteria was NOT discovered in the crawl knowledge, explicitly mark that step or test case evidence_status as "unsupported_missing_evidence" instead of inventing fake selectors or URLs.
